# Appendix B — Literate syntax and settings reference

This appendix is the lookup route for authored Literate syntax and the settings
that select host behavior. The language model behind the notation is developed in
[From explanation to source](from_explanation_to_source.html), while
[The project snapshot](project_snapshot.html) explains why document order and root
settings are semantic input rather than presentation details.

## Documents and project order

A project consists of every eligible `.literate` document beneath a workspace root,
not only the files linked by the index. Documents are interpreted in this order:

1. the normalized root `index.literate`, when present;
2. documents linked from that index, in link encounter order;
3. all remaining documents, sorted by normalized workspace-relative path.

An index link ending in `.html` selects the corresponding `.literate` source for
ordering. Query strings and fragments do not affect that selection, repeated links
do not insert a document twice, and unlinked chapters remain part of the project.
Because appends are assembled in project order, changing chapter order can change
generated source. See [The project snapshot](project_snapshot.html) and
[The fragment graph](fragment_graph.html).

## Fragment declarations

A named fragment is a fenced Markdown code block whose info string contains a
language, a colon, a name in double chevrons, and a definition operator. Whitespace
around the parts is permitted.

````text
```typescript : <<parse one request>>=
// fragment body
```
````

The language may be omitted by retaining the colon:

````text
``` : <<language-neutral text>>=
text
```
````

The first accepted declaration defines a fragment family. A second `=` definition
with the same exact name is a duplicate, not an implicit append. Names are exact and
case-sensitive; blank names and names containing angle brackets or line breaks are
invalid.

The opening fence may use any Markdown fence marker accepted by Markdown-it. The
fragment grammar applies to the complete info string, however, so unrelated trailing
text is not accepted as a comment.

## Ordered contributions

Use `=+` to append another contribution to a family that has already been defined:

````text
```typescript : <<parse one request>>=+
// later contribution
```
````

An append must encounter its base definition earlier in project order. It may carry
its own language label for presentation, but it cannot declare an output path or a
source template. Contributions are concatenated in deterministic encounter order.
Their separate source locations are retained for diagnostics, navigation, and
publication.

For the design consequences of definitions and appends, see
[Choosing fragments well](choosing_fragments.html) and
[Fragments as editor symbols](fragments_as_editor_symbols.html).

## References and expansion

Inside an accepted fragment body, a plain tag references another family:

```text
<<parse one request>>
```

References may precede their definitions in reading order. Tangling recursively
replaces each reference with the target family's ordered contributions. A reference
operator such as `=` or `=+` inside a body is invalid; definition operators belong in
fence headers.

When a reference appears after indentation on its line, each newline in the expanded
text receives that indentation. The indentation belongs to the use site, not to the
referenced family. Missing families, cycles, invalid operators, and expansion-limit
violations diagnose the project and prevent the complete generation plan.

To emit a literal opening delimiter from fragment code, escape it:

```text
\<<not a fragment reference>>
```

The escape prevents reference recognition, and the backslash is removed from the
generated and rendered code. Escaping is lexical: it applies to the opening chevrons,
not to a later matching name found by search.

## Output roots

A fragment whose exact name ends in `.*` is an output root. Its initial declaration
must name a workspace-relative destination followed by whitespace and `$`:

````text
```typescript : <<assemble the request parser.*>>= src/request-parser.ts $
<<parse one request>>
```
````

Only roots may declare destinations. Paths must remain within the project, satisfy
the portable path policy, and avoid collisions with source documents, templates,
HTML pages, other outputs, and ancestor file paths. A root without a destination is
invalid. Appending to a root uses only the ordinary append form; the destination is
not repeated.

Generation validates every root and dependency before returning any source or HTML
file. For expansion and all-or-nothing planning, see
[Planning a complete generation](complete_generation_plan.html). For the complete
root inventory, see [Appendix A — Program assembly maps](assembly.html).

## Source templates

A root may select one source template after its destination:

````text
```typescript : <<assemble the extension.*>>= src/extension.ts $ template=tssrc.template
<<extension body>>
```
````

The template path is workspace-relative and is loaded as declared project input.
Every original `[CODE]` marker is replaced with the complete tangled source. A
template without that marker remains valid but inserts no tangled code. Substitution
is literal and single-pass: inserted code is not scanned again for placeholders.
Wrapper text outside a `[CODE]` slot has no literate source mapping.

Only one `template` setting is accepted on a fragment declaration. Semicolons delimit
fragment settings, although `template` is currently the only recognized key. A
missing explicitly selected template is an error rather than a request to continue
without the wrapper.

## Root `SETTINGS` fences

Project-wide settings are ordinary fenced blocks whose trimmed info string is exactly
`SETTINGS`. They are hidden from rendered HTML and preview. Only settings in the
normalized root `index.literate` have project-wide authority; a chapter-local block
cannot override them.

````markdown
```SETTINGS
theme=rosepine-dawn
authors=First Author;Second Author
template=page.template
```
````

Settings use one `key=value` pair per line. Key matching is case-sensitive. Unknown
lines are ignored by the settings consumers rather than becoming a general extension
mechanism.

### Source and HTML settings

- `template` selects a workspace-relative HTML template. The template receives
  `[CONTENT]`, `[AUTHORS]`, and `[THEME]` by literal, single-pass substitution.
  `[CONTENT]` is rendered document content, `[AUTHORS]` is generated author metadata,
  and `[THEME]` is the complete selected document stylesheet.
- `authors` is a semicolon-separated author list used to construct HTML author
  metadata. A later recognized occurrence replaces the earlier value.
- `theme` selects an exact, case-sensitive name from the built-in catalogue. Omission
  uses the default catalogue theme. The last valid occurrence wins; every invalid
  occurrence remains a diagnostic. See [Document themes](themes.html) for names and
  color behavior.

Legacy HTML templates without `[THEME]` receive the stylesheet beside `[CONTENT]`.
An explicitly selected but unavailable HTML template is an error; the default page is
used only when no template was requested.

### Publication font settings

Print publication additionally recognizes these root keys:

- `font-body`
- `font-heading`
- `font-code`
- `font-fragment`
- `font-note`
- `font-code-ligatures`, whose value is exactly `on` or `off`

Font-family values are trimmed simple ASCII names: one to one hundred letters,
digits, spaces, dots, or hyphens, beginning with a letter or digit. They are family
names, not paths or TeX expressions. The last valid value for a role wins while
invalid occurrences remain diagnostics. If `font-note` is omitted, notes inherit the
final body family. Resolving these values does not install, locate, or bundle fonts.
See [Publication font settings](publication-settings.html) and
[Portable publication resources](portable_publication_resources.html).

## HTML template placeholders

HTML and source templates share one deliberately small substitution mechanism:

- source templates recognize `[CODE]`;
- HTML templates recognize `[CONTENT]`, `[AUTHORS]`, and `[THEME]`;
- matching original placeholders are replaced literally;
- inserted values are never scanned for more placeholders;
- unknown placeholders remain unchanged.

This is not a scripting or expression language. Template files are captured project
inputs and participate in freshness checks in the editor and language-service paths.

## Command-line reference

The headless adapter uses the same parser and generation engine as the extension.
Every command accepts an optional project root and `--json`; help is available through
`--help`, `-h`, or `help <command>`.

- `inspect` reports ordered documents, template dependencies, fragment declarations,
  roots, and references. It does not inspect output destinations.
- `check` constructs a complete valid plan and reports outputs as current, missing, or
  stale without writing.
- `generate` writes only missing or changed planned source and HTML outputs through the
  output transaction boundary.
- `self-check` verifies the repository's trusted self-hosting fixed point without
  generating files; planned outputs must already be current.
- `latex` requires `--output path.tex` and explicitly exports the publication model to
  LaTeX without generating source/HTML or compiling a PDF.
- `pdf` requires `--output path.pdf` and explicitly compiles in a private build
  directory, publishing only the requested PDF. `--keep-build` retains that private
  directory for inspection.

`latex` and `pdf` accept paper and margin options, `--font-manifest`, and an explicit
`--browser` path for Mermaid publication assets. Output paths are relative to the
selected project root. Consult [Command-line generation](cli.html) for the complete
flag grammar and structured response schema, and
[The command-line adapter](command_line_adapter.html) for effect boundaries.

In JSON mode the CLI emits one response object. Its `schemaVersion` is the automation
compatibility boundary; engine coordinates remain zero-based. Human output presents
one-based line and column numbers.

## Extension commands

The manifest exposes these stable command identifiers:

- `literate.process` — process every workspace project and commit valid changed
  generated outputs;
- `literate.create_fragment_for_tag` — create a family for the tag selected in the
  active document;
- `literate.split_fragment` — split a contribution and continue it as an append;
- `fragmentExplorer.refreshEntry` — refresh the Fragment Explorer view;
- `literate.restartLanguageServers` — dispose mapped-language sessions so later work
  starts fresh ones;
- `literate.exportLatex` and `literate.exportPdf` — begin explicit publication exports;
- `literate.cancelAutomaticCompletion` — cancel a pending automatic mapped-language
  request under its context-sensitive keybinding.

Command identifiers are compatibility boundaries for keybindings, tasks, and menus.
Their registration and effect levels are described in
[Extension activation and public surface](extension_activation_surface.html).

## Extension settings

### Mapped language services

- `literate.automaticCompletion` is a resource-scoped boolean, enabled by default. It
  controls automatic suggestions inside mapped fragment code; manual completion is
  unaffected.
- `literate.languageDiagnostics` is a resource-scoped boolean, enabled by default. It
  controls the separate mapped-language diagnostic collection, not Literate's own
  fragment and generation diagnostics.
- `literate.languageServers` is a restricted machine-scoped object read from global
  user settings. Keys are canonical VS Code language IDs. Each value requires an
  absolute `command` and may contain literal string `args`, object
  `initializationOptions`, and object `configuration`. No shell expansion occurs,
  workspace overrides are ignored, and servers are not installed automatically.

See [Owning a language-server session](owning_language_server_session.html) and
[Appendix E — Language-server protocol details](appendix_language_server_protocol.html).

### Publication tools

- `literate.publication.nodePath` selects the absolute external Node executable used
  by explicit publication work. Workspace overrides are ignored.
- `literate.publication.browserPath` optionally selects an installed, sandbox-capable
  Chromium executable for Mermaid publication. Literate does not download a browser.
- `literate.publication.fontManifest` selects an optional manifest path relative to
  the workspace folder. It remains subject to publication path and license checks.

These are restricted machine settings because they influence external process or
resource boundaries. See [Isolating publication from the editor](isolating_publication_editor.html).

## Common failure lookup

- **A declaration is ignored:** check that the complete fence info string matches the
  fragment header grammar and that the name is nonempty.
- **An append is rejected:** its base definition must occur earlier, and the append
  must not repeat output or template settings.
- **A root produces no file:** a root name must end in `.*`, declare a nonempty
  destination terminated by `$`, and belong to a project with no diagnostics.
- **A tag is copied literally:** it may be escaped, outside an accepted fragment body,
  or shown in ordinary Markdown rather than used as a reference.
- **A setting has no effect:** verify exact case, an exact `SETTINGS` info string, and
  placement in the normalized root `index.literate`.
- **No files are returned:** generation is intentionally all-or-nothing; inspect every
  diagnostic, including errors in unreachable families and templates.
